# The ledger of `msgpack`: what MessagePack's specification (msgpack.org,
# `spec.md` of the `msgpack/msgpack` repository, read 2026-11-16) lets a
# program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. None of the
# three reference libraries holds MessagePack in its standard library:
# `go doc -all` lists no such package in Go 1.27, Rust 1.100's `std` has none,
# and Python 3.15 has no `msgpack` module, nor is one installed on this
# machine. So every block's `go`, `rust` and `python` lines are `-`, and the
# capabilities are the specification's own; the expected bytes of the tests
# are its format tables', and floats' bits were observed from Python's
# `struct` (`>f`, `>d`).

capability msgpack/decode  one encoded object read into a value a program matches on
  heroes  msgpack.decode, msgpack.Value, msgpack.Entry
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every format of the specification's table is read; `0xc1`, which it never uses, is refused, and a count or a length the remaining bytes could not hold fails before anything is held for it

capability msgpack/decode-options  how strictly bytes are read: depth, a key twice
  heroes  msgpack.decode_with, msgpack.strict, msgpack.DecodeOptions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the specification says nothing of a key twice; `strict` refuses it, as CBOR calls such a map not valid, two keys being one where their smallest encodings are, so `0xd001` and `0x01` are the same key

capability msgpack/raw-text  a string whose bytes are not UTF-8 handed back as its bytes
  heroes  msgpack.DecodeOptions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the specification asks a deserializer to make the original bytes reachable; `invalid_text` reads such a string as a `.binary`, and `strict` refuses it `not_text`

capability msgpack/sequence  objects written one after another, read in turn
  heroes  msgpack.decode_next, msgpack.Decoded
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `decode` refuses bytes after its object and names where they begin

capability msgpack/encode  a value written, each object in its smallest format
  heroes  msgpack.encode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the specification's SHOULD: positive fixint, uint 8 to 64 and negative fixint, int 8 to 64 of fewest bytes; fix, 8, 16 or 32-bit headers; fixext where the length is 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16; float 32 where a single holds the double exactly; a map holding one key twice, a `.negative` not below 0 and a length past 2^32 - 1 fail `invalid`

capability msgpack/key-order  a map's entries sorted, so equal values encode to equal bytes
  heroes  msgpack.encode_with, msgpack.KeyOrder
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    MessagePack names no deterministic encoding; `.bytewise` takes the order CBOR's deterministic encoding uses, the keys' encodings compared byte by byte

capability msgpack/integers  every MessagePack integer held, made from and read as an `i64`
  heroes  msgpack.from_int, msgpack.int_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `.unsigned` holds 0 to 2^64 - 1 and `.negative` -2^63 to -1, so a value read from any of the eight integer formats is held one way only

capability msgpack/floats  float 32 and float 64 read, and written at their smallest
  heroes  msgpack.Value
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every float is held as an `f64`, which holds every single exactly; the bits are read and made by `numbers`' exact conversions (`bits_to_f64`, `bits_to_f32`, `f64_to_bits`, `f32_to_bits`) since 2026-11-20, every NaN written as the one quiet NaN as `bson` writes it; until then by exact arithmetic on powers of two in a module of this package's own, whose opening comment said no package below offered a float's bits, which `numbers` did

capability msgpack/float-width  a float written in 64 bits though a single holds it
  heroes  refused: the specification asks a serializer for the smallest format, and the value holds a number, not the width it came in; every reader of MessagePack reads both widths
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability msgpack/extensions  an extension's type and bytes kept
  heroes  msgpack.Value
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `.extension` keeps any type from -128 to 127 and its bytes, so an extension the program does not know is carried through untouched, as the specification allows

capability msgpack/timestamp  the timestamp extension read as an instant and written from one
  heroes  msgpack.instant_of, msgpack.from_instant, msgpack.TIMESTAMP
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the three layouts of 32, 64 and 96 bits, written in the smallest as the specification's pseudo code chooses; nanoseconds past 999999999 fail `invalid`

capability msgpack/timestamp-wide  a timestamp outside 1677 to 2262 read
  heroes  refused: a `time.Instant` holds nanoseconds in an `i64`, 1677 to 2262; `instant_of` fails `does_not_fit` past it, and the seconds of the 96-bit layout stand in the extension's bytes for a program that needs them
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability msgpack/json  a MessagePack object crossed to a JSON value and back
  heroes  msgpack.to_json, msgpack.from_json
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    what JSON cannot hold, a binary, an extension, a key that is not a string, a `nan` or an infinity, fails `unsupported` with the JSON pointer of the object, and an integer past 2^53 - 1 fails `does_not_fit`; a JSON number that is a whole number from -2^63 to 2^64 - 1 becomes an integer, every other one a float

capability msgpack/member  a map's value read by its string key
  heroes  msgpack.member
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the first entry where a key stands twice

capability msgpack/read-kind  an object's kind, truth, float, string, bytes, array items and map entries read out, each failing where the object is of another kind
  heroes  msgpack.kind_of, msgpack.bool_of, msgpack.float_of, msgpack.text_of, msgpack.bytes_of, msgpack.items_of, msgpack.entries_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-20, `cbor`'s readers by the same names (cbor/read-kind), so a program moved from CBOR to MessagePack by its `use` line keeps its reads; each fails `invalid` naming the kind it expected and the kind it found in MessagePack's words; a string is no binary, an extension's bytes are no binary, and an integer no float, which `int_of` reads; `kind_of` answers the case's name as a `match` writes it

capability msgpack/records  a record of the program written to and read from a map by its field names
  heroes  refused: the language has no reflection; a record is written by hand with its `Record::field` names (spec § 9), which the compiler checks, as `cbor`'s example of `encode` shows, and a generator of those functions would be a verb of `heroes`, which needs a design sitting
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability msgpack/stream  objects read from and written to a stream as they come
  heroes  refused: `io` reads the bytes and writes them; `decode_next` walks objects held whole, and `msgpack` takes no stream
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
